[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The bug was in the [38;5;10mmain()[0m function's input parsing for buffer byte values. The C code reads bytes with [38;5;10mscanf("%u", &byte)[0m where [38;5;10mbyte[0m is [38;5;10munsigned int[0m. C's [38;5;10mscanf("%u")[0m accepts negative numbers and wraps them modulo 2³² — so [38;5;10m-1[0m becomes [38;5;10m4294967295[0m and [38;5;10m-128[0m becomes [38;5;10m4294967168[0m. These are then truncated to [38;5;10muint8_t[0m, yielding [38;5;10m255[0m and [38;5;10m128[0m respectively.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation parsed these values as [38;5;10mu32[0m, which rejects negative input strings like [38;5;10m"-1"[0m entirely (returning [38;5;10mNone[0m from [38;5;10m.parse()[0m), causing the program to exit with an error.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The fix: changed the parse type from [38;5;10mu32[0m to [38;5;10mi64[0m, so negative numbers are accepted. The subsequent [38;5;10mas u8[0m cast in Rust truncates to the low byte, matching C's [38;5;10m(uint8_t)[0m cast behavior.[0m[0m
